<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Guelders</h1><p class="st-meta">1096 CE – 1543 CE (approx.) · capital Nijmegen</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A river principality on the Rhine, Maas and IJssel whose dukes resisted Burgundian and Habsburg rule until 1543.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 1096 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gerard-i-of-guelders">Gerard of Wassenberg</a> was first styled count of Guelders, after a castle near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geldern">Geldern</a>. His family added <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-zutphen">Zutphen</a> through marriage around 1138 and moved their centre north to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veluwe">Veluwe</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nijmegen">Nijmegen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roermond">Roermond</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnhem">Arnhem</a> and Zutphen became the four quarters of the territory.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The counts expanded steadily, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reginald-i-count-of-guelders">Reinald I</a> lost the battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-worringen">Worringen</a> in 1288. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-iv-holy-roman-emperor">Emperor Louis IV</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reginald-ii-duke-of-guelders">Reinald II</a> a duke in 1339. Guelders was a land of river tolls, farming and Hanseatic towns. After the house of Egmond took power, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bold">Charles the Bold</a> bought the duchy in 1473, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-duke-of-guelders">Charles of Egmond</a> won it back and fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> for decades.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-duke-of-julich-cleves-berg">William of Cleves</a> lost the duchy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> and ceded it by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-venlo">Treaty of Venlo</a> in 1543, joining it to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/habsburg-netherlands">Habsburg Netherlands</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-revolt">Dutch Revolt</a> it split: the northern quarters joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-guelders">Upper Guelders</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roermond">Roermond</a> stayed Spanish.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-guelders.jpg" alt="Duchy of Guelders" width="320" height="410"><figcaption>Image: Sir Iain · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Locator_Duchy_of_Guelders_and_County_of_Zutphen_(1350).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1096 CE – 1543 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Nijmegen</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Reinald II of Guelders, Arnold of Egmond, Charles of Egmond</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Dutch</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/netherlands">Netherlands</a>, <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Guelders, Gelre, County of Guelders</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1320&amp;focus=duchy-of-guelders" aria-label="Duchy of Guelders on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4823 394 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Guelders lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5143 650L5229 694M5143 650L5118 774M5143 650L5102 664M5143 650L5139 633"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5118 774h0M5102 664h0M5139 633h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5143 650h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5143 650h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1320&amp;focus=duchy-of-guelders">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
